A||N||11||Gold-86SE||Radiator Fan Trouble||||1||
Z||000000||Gold-86SE||07-10-2001||10:56 AM||||<BR>Okay here is the deal. After I arrived at work this morning, I turned off the Fiero and the radiator fan continued to run.<P>By the reading on the gauge, the temperature was no where near hot.  So, I don't believe the sensor turned on the fan.  Therefore, I removed every fuse trying to stop the fan, but no dice.  I tried turning on the car and turning off the a/c, no change to the fan. The only thing that stopped the fan was the removal of the negative battery cable.<P>Has anyone experienced this? Does anyone have a clue what would cause this?<P><P>------------------<BR>84 SE 4.10 4 Speed (Not So Fair Weather Driver)<BR>85 GT Automatic (Restoration Project)<BR>86 SE 4 Speed (Fair Weather Daily Driver)<BR>87 SC 5 Speed (Not Running, V8 Conversion?)<BR>Please visit my <A HREF="http://www.trifocus.net/~fws/index.html" TARGET=_blank>Fiero Work Shop</A>||||reg||1
Z||000001||West Coast Fiero||07-10-2001||11:42 AM||||it is common for the fan to run nearly 1 to 2 seconds after you shut of the car during its normal operation, however does it continue to run long after you shut the car off?||||reg||1
Z||000002||Steven||07-10-2001||11:45 AM||||You did pull the #4 (20 amp) fuse? If so, the first thing I would look at would be the cooling fan relay. Mounted in front of the drivers side headlight.||||reg||5
Z||000003||Steven||07-10-2001||12:00 PM||||You did pull the #4 (20 amp) fuse? If so, the first thing I would look at would be the cooling fan relay. Mounted in front of the drivers side headlight.||||reg||5
Z||000004||Gold-86SE||07-10-2001||12:15 PM||||From West Coast Fiero<BR>Q. Does it continue to run long after you shut the car off?<P>A. Yes. I went to retrieve a tool so I could take the neg. bat. cable off and it ran the entire time, which was about 3 minutes, maybe a little longer. <P><BR>From Steven<BR>Q. Did pull the #4 (20 amp) fuse?<P>A. Yes. Then I pulled each fuse trying to kill the fan motor, and it was a no go.<P><BR>Additionally, Steven mentioned the cooling fan relay mounted in front of the drivers side headlight, I'll have to check this out.  How do I know if it is not working?<P>Furthermore, I have never experienced the raditor fan running while the Fiero is turned off.  That is why I am perplexed. Hopefully it is something simple.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Carrolles||07-10-2001||12:29 PM||||Assuming fuse #4 provides power to the signal side of the relay then I'd guess the relay is stuck in the ON position getting it's power directly from the battery.<P>Try giving the relay a hard rap to see if you jog it to unlatch itself.||||reg||1
Z||000006||sqoach||07-10-2001||12:58 PM||||Yes, I've had this problem before. Turned the car off and fan would just stay on. I just unplugged it from the fan motor for a temporary fix.<BR>Check the relay. Unplug it, clean it up, and plug it back in. How old is the relay?<P>------------------<BR>sqoach@yahoo.com<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/forumsig.JPG">      <BR>84 SE (FOR SALE)<BR>85 Coupe (parts car)(take it off my hands for $50)<BR>86 SC (FOR SALE sometime in this summer)<BR>86 SE V6 (new project)||||reg||1
Z||000007||mbramble||07-10-2001||01:28 PM||||Fuse for the cooling fan relay is at the top right of the fuse panel (labeled C/H) and according to the owner's manual should be a 10a fuse.  If you pulled that one and the fan continues to blow, it would almost have to be a stuck relay.  Power for the fan itself comes thru a fusible link (then thru the relay) and is not actually fused so if the relay is stuck, you could pull all the fuses and the fan will stay on.<P>As suggested above, tap the relay with something to see if it will 'unstick'.  I don't recall if you can get the cover off the relay to clean the contacts or not.  If not, you should probably try to find a replacement.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Gold-86SE||07-10-2001||02:08 PM||||<BR>Well as suggested, I while at lunch, tapped the relay and then reconnected my neg. bat. cable, the fan did not run so I assumed it fixed the problem.<P>On my way back to the office at the end of lunch I had to go through a couple of intersections, and the temperature was climbing.  When I arrive at the parking lot, the temp was almost 220.<P>I pop the hood get out of the Fiero and notice that the fan is not running.  I went from bad to worse.  This sucks and it is supposed to hit almost 100* today.  Good thing I don't have to drive very far to get home tonight.<P>I think it is a relay problem. I don't know the age of the relay, but I think I have an extra in the garage.<P>Thanks for the help guys.  I'll let you guys know later, if a relay replacement fixes or doesn't fix the problem.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000009||West Coast Fiero||07-10-2001||03:00 PM||||it is possible that your fan switch curcuit has a short, that the switch is malfunctioning, of that the fan relay is stuck closed||||reg||1
Z||000010||Steven||07-10-2001||03:07 PM||||Let me say this first. I would only do this if I really had to. If you unplug your relay, and use a large paper clip to jumper the red wire with the black w/red strip wire. These are the two thick gauge wires in the connector. This will force your blower motor to be on the whole way home. Once home pull the jumper out. It will be warm to the touch, and then you can install your extra relay that you have at home. <p>[This message has been edited by Steven (edited 07-10-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000011||Gold-86SE||07-10-2001||06:34 PM||||<BR>Steven, thanks for the tip.  That worked great.<P>Arrived at the house and replaced the relay with a spare.  Everything works fine.<P>Thanks guys.<BR>And a special thanks to Steven for giving me a temporary fix so the fan would operate so I did not run the risk of overheating.<BR>||||reg||1
